/**
 * \page guide_contiguous Contiguous containers
 * \tableofcontents
 *
 * One contiguous buffer — on the heap (`Array`, and the `PriorityQueue` adapter
 * over it) or inline in the object (`StaticArray`, `FixedArray`) — laid out so the
 * elements pack tightly for cache-friendly iteration and O(1) index addressing.
 * Growth relocates the live range into a fresh buffer, so iterators/references are
 * *not* stable across a grow (and across mid-sequence insert/erase, which shift
 * elements); the fixed/inline types never reallocate. A trivially-relocatable
 * element type grows via a single `memmove` instead of a move+destroy loop.
 *
 * \section gc_array Array<T>
 * Dynamic, contiguous, allocator-aware sequence — the engine's `vector`,
 * insertion-ordered. (R42, R46)
 *
 * - **Memory:** one heap buffer, `{begin, end, capacity}` pointers plus the
 *   (often empty) allocator (EBO); split across `ArrayBase` (RAII storage) and
 *   `Array` (element lifetime).
 * - **Complexity:** lookup O(n) scan / O(1) `operator[]`/`at`; insert `pushBack`/
 *   `emplaceBack` amortized O(1), `insert`/`emplace` mid-sequence O(n) (shift);
 *   erase O(n), `eraseUnsorted` O(1) (swap-with-last).
 * - **Iterators / stability:** raw `T*`; invalidated on any grow (`pushBack` past
 *   capacity, `reserve`, `shrinkToFit`, reallocating `insert`); `erase` invalidates
 *   from the erased position onward.
 * - **Use when:** the default sequence — append-and-iterate workloads, indexed
 *   access, unordered bags (pair with `eraseUnsorted`).
 * - **Avoid when:** you need references/iterators to survive edits (use a node
 *   list), or frequent mid-sequence insert/erase (O(n) shift).
 * - **Notes:** trivially-relocatable elements grow via one `memmove`
 *   (`memory_util::relocate`). `pushBack`/`emplaceBack` are force-inlined with the
 *   grow path inline, so the optimizer promotes the begin/end/capacity members into
 *   registers across a caller's push loop — parity with `std::vector`/EASTL (R42).
 *   Capacity is hard-capped at `kMaxElements`, and `getNewCapacity` saturates to it
 *   instead of wrapping `SizeType` (R46). Reserve up front to make a push burst
 *   realloc-free.
 *
 * \code{.cpp}
 * using namespace worse::core::container;
 *
 * Array<int> xs;
 * xs.reserve(1024);            // one allocation; the loop never reallocates
 * for (int i = 0; i < 1024; ++i)
 * {
 *     xs.pushBack(i);          // amortized O(1), force-inlined
 * }
 * int const total = xs.size() ? xs[xs.size() - 1] : 0; // O(1) index
 * xs.eraseUnsorted(xs.begin()); // O(1) unordered removal
 * \endcode
 *
 * Full API: \ref worse::core::container::Array
 *
 * \section gc_static StaticArray<T,N>
 * Fixed-size inline aggregate of exactly `N` elements — the engine's `std::array`,
 * no allocator, no heap, fully `constexpr`.
 *
 * - **Memory:** `N` elements stored directly in the object;
 *   `sizeof(StaticArray<T,N>) == sizeof(T[N])` (zero overhead). Aggregate: a public
 *   `mData[N]` and no user-declared constructors, so `{...}` initialization works.
 * - **Complexity:** lookup O(n) scan / O(1) `operator[]`/`at`; no insert/erase —
 *   the size is fixed at `N`.
 * - **Iterators / stability:** raw `T*`; **stable** — the storage never moves, so
 *   references/iterators stay valid for the object's lifetime.
 * - **Use when:** `N` is a compile-time constant and you want a value-type buffer
 *   with zero overhead and stable storage (math vectors, lookup tables, small
 *   fixed records).
 * - **Avoid when:** the count is only known at runtime, or you need to grow/shrink
 *   (use `FixedArray` for a bounded runtime size, `Array` for unbounded).
 * - **Notes:** bounds are checked by a debug-only `WE_ASSERT`; out of range in a
 *   release build is UB (the engine's no-throw contract). `static_assert(N > 0)`.
 *
 * \code{.cpp}
 * using namespace worse::core::container;
 *
 * StaticArray<int, 3> a{1, 2, 3}; // aggregate init; no heap, no ctor call
 * a[1] = 20;                      // O(1), bounds-checked in debug
 * int const sum = a[0] + a[1] + a[2];
 * \endcode
 *
 * Full API: \ref worse::core::container::StaticArray
 *
 * \section gc_fixed FixedArray<T,N>
 * Fixed-capacity array with a *runtime* size — the engine's `fixed_vector`: up to
 * `N` elements in an inline, aligned byte buffer, zero heap allocation. (R6, R46)
 *
 * - **Memory:** an inline `alignas(T)` byte buffer sized for `N` elements plus a
 *   `usize mSize`; no allocator (element lifetime via `construct_at`/`destroy_at`).
 * - **Complexity:** lookup O(n) scan / O(1) `operator[]`/`at`; `pushBack`/
 *   `emplaceBack` O(1) until full; `insert`/`emplace` mid-sequence O(n) (shift);
 *   erase O(n), `eraseUnsorted` O(1).
 * - **Iterators / stability:** raw `T*`; the buffer never moves, but `insert`/
 *   `erase` shift elements and so invalidate from the touched position onward.
 * - **Use when:** a bounded, zero-heap sequence whose length varies at runtime —
 *   per-frame scratch, fixed-cap work queues, small-buffer collections.
 * - **Avoid when:** the bound is unknown or can be exceeded as normal flow (use
 *   `Array`); the plain push/insert/resize paths *abort* on overflow.
 * - **Notes:** overflow is fail-closed — `pushBack`/`emplaceBack`/`insert`/`resize`
 *   and the count-taking ctors abort via always-on `WE_VERIFY` in *every* build,
 *   no heap spill (R46). For the non-aborting path use `tryPushBack` (→ `bool`) /
 *   `tryEmplaceBack` (→ pointer, `nullptr` when full), and pre-check with `full()`/
 *   `remaining()`. Because `mSize` is an index, not a self-pointer, `FixedArray<T,N>`
 *   is trivially relocatable exactly when `T` is (R7), so an
 *   `Array<FixedArray<...>>` grows by `memcpy`.
 *
 * \code{.cpp}
 * using namespace worse::core::container;
 *
 * FixedArray<int, 8> buf; // inline, cap 8, no heap
 * for (int i = 0; i < 100; ++i)
 * {
 *     if (!buf.tryPushBack(i)) // non-aborting: false once full() at 8
 *     {
 *         break;
 *     }
 * }
 * // buf.pushBack(99); // would ABORT here (size() == N) — use tryPushBack instead
 * \endcode
 *
 * Full API: \ref worse::core::container::FixedArray
 *
 * \section gc_pqueue PriorityQueue<T>
 * Container adapter keeping a backing sequence (default `Array<T>`) arranged as a
 * binary **max-heap**, so the highest-priority element is always at the front —
 * the engine's `std::priority_queue`. (R45)
 *
 * - **Memory:** none of its own — just policy over the backing `Container` (heap,
 *   over `Array<T>` by default), so it inherits that container's allocator and
 *   relocation behaviour.
 * - **Complexity:** `top` O(1); `push`/`emplace` O(log n) (append then sift up);
 *   `pop` O(log n) (rotate root to back, re-sift, drop). Range/`initializer_list`
 *   seeding heapifies in one O(n) pass.
 * - **Iterators / stability:** none exposed — access is `top`/`push`/`pop` only.
 * - **Use when:** you always want the current best element next — open sets (A*),
 *   timer wheels, scheduled-event queues.
 * - **Avoid when:** you need to iterate in priority order without draining, random
 *   access, or a deterministic order among equal priorities (see below).
 * - **Notes:** `reserve`/`capacity`/`getAllocator` forward to the backing `Array`,
 *   so a known push burst is pre-sizable and realloc-free (R45). Default `Less<>`
 *   gives a max-heap (`Greater<>` for a min-heap). Caveat: a binary heap is **not
 *   stable** — equal-priority elements pop in an *unspecified* order that can differ
 *   across runs/platforms (a determinism footgun for replay/netcode); fold a
 *   sequence/insertion counter into the priority for a stable tiebreak.
 *
 * \code{.cpp}
 * using namespace worse::core::container;
 *
 * PriorityQueue<int> pq;
 * pq.reserve(256);     // pre-size the backing Array; the push burst is realloc-free
 * pq.push(3);
 * pq.push(9);
 * pq.push(5);
 * int const best = pq.top(); // 9 (max-heap), O(1)
 * pq.pop();                  // removes 9, O(log n)
 * \endcode
 *
 * Full API: \ref worse::core::container::PriorityQueue
 *
 * \section gc_pick When to pick which
 *
 * - **`Array`** — the default. Contiguous storage, indexed access, append-and-iterate;
 *   reserve up front to make a push burst realloc-free.
 * - **`StaticArray<T,N>`** — when `N` is a compile-time constant and you want a
 *   zero-overhead value buffer with stable storage (it never moves).
 * - **`FixedArray<T,N>`** — when you need a bounded, zero-heap sequence with a
 *   *runtime* size; fails closed at capacity, with `tryPushBack`/`tryEmplaceBack`
 *   escape hatches.
 * - **`PriorityQueue`** — when you only ever want the current best element next:
 *   open sets, timers, event queues.
 */
